    "available_at": "2026-11-16T23:10:07.830410+00:00",
    "body": "The Port Authority of New York and New Jersey is proposing road improvements, wharf rehabilitation and replacement public berths at Port Newark as part of its $10.3 billion capital and operating budget for 2027. The spending plan also includes $21 million for maintenance dredging to keep the port’s commercial shipping channels navigable.\nThe investments would support a port complex projected to handle 9 million twenty-foot equivalent units, or TEUs, in 2027. The Port of New York and New Jersey is the East Coast’s busiest container port.\nThe proposed capital budget introduced at the bistate agency’s recent meeting would advance key components of the Port Street Corridor Improvement project, an ongoing effort to modernize the road network serving Port Newark. The work would build on the opening of the Corbin Street Ramp, which the agency identified as a major milestone in the corridor project during 2026.\nOn the waterfront, the plan calls for wharf rehabilitation to continue at multiple facilities and construction to begin on replacement public berths at Port Newark. These projects address the infrastructure used to accommodate vessels and support cargo handling, complementing the road improvements on the landside of the port.\nSeparately, the proposed operating budget includes $21 million for maintenance dredging of the commercial shipping channels serving the port. That allocation places upkeep of existing maritime access alongside longer-term improvements to roads and waterfront structures.\n“The Port Authority has a responsibility to invest in the future while taking care of the infrastructure that keeps it moving today,” Chairman Kevin O’Toole said in outlining the agency’s investment and maintenance priorities.\nAcross all Port Authority facilities, the proposed budget includes $4 billion in annual capital spending, $4.5 billion in operating expenses, $1.7 billion for debt service and $100 million in deferred expenses. The wider program also funds airport, passenger rail, bus and other transportation infrastructure.\nThe 2027 spending plan remains subject to public input and final board action. Commissioners are scheduled to meet December 17 for a vote.\nWhy it matters: The proposed investments address three connected parts of port operations: Channels that provide vessel access, berths and wharves that support waterfront activity, and roads that connect terminals at the busiest East Coast gateway.\nRead more articles by Stuart Chirls here .\nRead more:\nU.S. container imports hit record 2.67 million TEUs in October as port delays rise\nTanker strikes cruise riverboat in New Orleans\nCMA CGM triples profit as Q3 freight rates and cargo volumes rise\nEvergreen Marine nine-month profit up 18% to $2.16 billion\nWhite House to extend Jones Act waiver as deadline nears, sources say",
